language ESSENCE' 1.0
$ best_host: an Essence' model from DCP Rosetta.
$
$ Six dinner guests are seated around a round table: Andrew, Betty, Cara, Dave, Erica and Frank. Andrew will only sit next to Dave and Frank; Betty will only sit next to Cara and Erica; Cara will only sit next to Betty and Frank; Dave will only sit next to Andrew and Erica; Erica will only sit next to Betty and Dave; Frank will only sit next to Andrew and Cara. Arrange the guests in a seating order around the table so that every guest sits only next to the guests they get along with, which is a seating without any conflict.
$
$   https://dcp-bench.github.io/DCP-Rosetta/problems/best_host.html
$ Save this model as model.eprime and run it with Savile Row 1.11 (it needs Java); the solution is
$ written to `model.eprime.solution`:
$   savilerow model.eprime -run-solver

$ x[k]: the guest in seat k going round the table, seats 1 to 6; guests are coded 0 Andrew, 1 Betty, 2 Cara, 3 Dave, 4 Erica, 5 Frank
find x : matrix indexed by [int(1..6)] of int(0..5)

$ compatible: the ordered pairs of guests who get along, in both orders (each guest's listed neighbours)
letting compatible = [[0,3],[0,5],[1,2],[1,4],[2,1],[2,5],[3,0],[3,4],[4,1],[4,3],[5,0],[5,2]]

branching on [x]
such that
$ each guest sits in exactly one seat
allDiff(x),
$ the guests in seats 1 and 2 get along
table([x[1], x[2]], compatible),
$ the guests in seats 2 and 3 get along
table([x[2], x[3]], compatible),
$ the guests in seats 3 and 4 get along
table([x[3], x[4]], compatible),
$ the guests in seats 4 and 5 get along
table([x[4], x[5]], compatible),
$ the guests in seats 5 and 6 get along
table([x[5], x[6]], compatible),
$ the guests in seats 6 and 1 get along (the table is round)
table([x[6], x[1]], compatible)
